**More on Standardization**
So here I wanted to write a bit about the problems with standardization. With commercial and open source LLMs, their way of "going about" things is different. What I mean by that is, the final attributes of a certain data entry may differ in name. For example, PEG (Polyethylene Glycol) could be written as poly(ethylene glycol) or PEO (Polyethylene oxide), which is still a part of the PEG family. 

One of the more interesting problems I ran into: a solvent list like "2-octanol / 1,2-dichlorobenzene" was getting split into 3 different conditions. THere was nothing in the code to tell the LLM to not split it, so it saw this as multiple compacted results. 

However, despite these problems I encountered, my pipeline's PEG, PLA, and PPO extractions against the hand-curated tables gives F1 scores (a standard measure combining precision and recall) in the 0.52–0.56 range overall. It's ok for a first try, but that is very average. I am in the process of building chemical tables into the code to ensure I don't have to hand code each entry in. Regardless, determining exactly how two terms are strictly "the same" is still a answer we are working towards. I have a presentation at SERMACS from November 4-7, so I'll be working towards that.periodically update this blog post with more updates.

